# ParticleMan Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is ParticleMan, a framework for pre-training foundation models for particle physics tasks.

## Development Guidelines

### Code Style and Quality
- Use Python 3.12 as the minimum version
- Follow PEP 8 and use black for formatting (line length: 88)
- Use ruff for fast linting
- Sort imports with isort (black profile)
- Use flake8 for additional linting
- Maintain type hints for all functions and classes
- Use mypy for static type checking

### Project Structure
- Use src/ layout: all code goes in src/particleman/
- Tests go in src/particleman/tests/
- Follow modular, readable, and safe coding practices
- Keep functions small and focused
- Use descriptive variable and function names

### Dependencies and Environment
- Use conda for environment management
- All dependencies should be specified in pyproject.toml
- Pin minimum versions for stability
- Core dependencies: torch, numpy, pandas, scikit-learn
- Dev dependencies: pytest, black, ruff, mypy, pre-commit

### Testing
- Use pytest for all testing
- Maintain high test coverage
- Tests should be in src/particleman/tests/
- Use descriptive test names
- Test both happy path and edge cases

### Security
- Never use eval() without proper sandboxing
- Validate all inputs, especially from external sources
- Use bandit for security scanning
- Be cautious with pickle and other serialization formats

### Documentation
- Use docstrings for all public functions, classes, and modules
- Follow Google or NumPy docstring style
- Keep README.md updated with usage examples
- Document complex algorithms and data transformations

### Git and Collaboration
- Use meaningful commit messages
- Run pre-commit hooks before committing
- All code must pass CI checks before merging
- Use feature branches for development

### Performance
- Profile code for performance bottlenecks
- Use vectorized operations where possible (numpy, torch)
- Consider memory usage for large datasets
- Use appropriate data types (float32 vs float64)

### Machine Learning Specific
- Use proper train/validation/test splits
- Implement reproducible experiments (set seeds)
- Log experiments with wandb or tensorboard
- Use configuration management (hydra/omegaconf)
- Validate model inputs and outputs
- Handle missing data appropriately

### Code Review Checklist
- [ ] Code follows style guidelines (black, ruff, isort)
- [ ] All functions have type hints
- [ ] Tests are written and pass
- [ ] Documentation is updated
- [ ] No security vulnerabilities
- [ ] Performance considerations addressed
- [ ] Error handling is appropriate
- [ ] Code is modular and reusable

### Common Patterns
- Use dataclasses for structured data
- Use enums for constants and options
- Use context managers for resource management
- Use logging instead of print statements
- Use pathlib for file system operations
- Use f-strings for string formatting

### Avoid
- Global variables (use configuration instead)
- Hard-coded paths or values
- Deep nesting (max 4 levels)
- Long functions (>50 lines)
- Mutable default arguments
- Using star imports (from module import *)

### File Organization
```
src/particleman/
├── __init__.py          # Package initialization with version
├── core/                # Core functionality (when needed)
├── models/              # Model definitions (when needed)
├── data/                # Data handling utilities (when needed)
├── training/            # Training logic (when needed)
├── utils/               # Utility functions (when needed)
└── tests/               # All tests
    ├── __init__.py
    ├── test_*.py        # Test files
    └── conftest.py      # Test configuration (when needed)
```

### Pre-commit Hooks
The following tools run automatically on commit:
- ruff (linting and formatting)
- black (code formatting)
- isort (import sorting)
- flake8 (additional linting)
- mypy (type checking)
- pytest (tests)
- bandit (security scanning) 